# `var result = $newArrayLike(length); for (; i < bound && $hasContiguousIndex(array, i); i++) if (!$tryPutByValDirect(result, i, { held: $getOwnContiguousIndex(array, i) })) break;` fills an array that was allocated with a length.
# The iterations run no other code and write no array's storage. So the indexing type of the array that is filled is checked once before the specialized version, and its storage is read once there.
# Where the index is below the length that the array was allocated with, the store is PutContiguousIndex and its result is true, so the code for a false result is not built (f1: the bound is that length).
# Fault injection, testing=AssumeIndexesInBounds: every index from zero up counts as in bounds (f2: the bound is a parameter). In a default build the loop would store past the end of the vector.
# With testing=CheckBoundsAtRunTime the same operation is built, and what the walk vouches for is checked against the array: the process crashes with a named error.
# RUN(EVENT): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# EVENT: = HasArrayStorage [[list]] arrayStorage=Contiguous [isWritable]
# EVENT: isLoopHead=true
# EVENT-NOT: TryPutByValDirect
# EVENT: = PutContiguousIndex [[list]], {{*}} storage=[[storage]]
# EVENT: [[storage]]: {{*}} = GetArrayStorage [[list]]
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.after = () => print(JSON.stringify(copy.call(["a", {}, "c"], null, 0)), JSON.stringify(upTo.call(["a", {}, "c"], null, 2)), JSON.stringify(upTo.call(["a", {}, "c"], null, 1000)));
